# negative_literal_test.deor
# Documents a real, currently-existing gap: unary minus has no handling in
# codegen/decl/stmt/expr/macros/prefix_ops.deor (only move/avow/not are
# handled there), so a negative literal falls all the way through to
# primary.deor's "/* unknown_primary */" fallback. The Deor-level validator
# doesn't catch this — it transpiles "successfully" — but the generated Rust
# is `let mut val: i64 = /* unknown_primary */;` with the actual "5" left
# over as a stray unhandled token, which rustc then rejects. This test
# captures that actual behavior so a future fix (or a regression making it
# worse) shows up here instead of going unnoticed. If unary minus ever gets
# real codegen support, this test should be rewritten as `# expect:` with
# the correct negative value printed.

fn void main()
	int val = -5
	print(val)

# expect-rustc-error:
# expected expression, found `;`
